The base type promises `save()` always works for any `Bird`:

```java
class Bird {
    void fly() { /* takes off and flies */ }
}
class Penguin extends Bird {
    void fly() {
        throw new UnsupportedOperationException("Penguins can't fly");
    }
}
```

Any code written against `Bird` — `for (Bird b : birds) b.fly();` — crashes the moment a `Penguin` is in the list, even though it type-checks perfectly. The fix is to stop modeling "is-a" from the English word and model it from behavior:

```java
interface Bird {}
interface FlyingBird extends Bird {
    void fly();
}
class Sparrow implements FlyingBird {
    public void fly() { /* ... */ }
}
class Penguin implements Bird {}
```

Now only birds that can actually fly implement `fly()`, and `Penguin` never has to lie about a capability it doesn't have.
